    <article class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-8 shadow-xl mt-12 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      
      <div class="border-b border-slate-800 pb-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">
          Analytical Titration Theory, Henderson-Hasselbalch, and Equivalence Points
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mt-1">
          A rigorous physicochemical breakdown of stoichiometric neutralization, buffer capacities, and first-derivative peak inflection.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-cyan-300">1. Mathematical Treatment of Titration Stages</h3>
        <p class="text-sm">
          A potentiometric acid-base titration is categorized into four distinct equilibrium zones as standard titrant volume (V) is systematically delivered from a calibrated burette into an analyte solution:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs">
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="font-bold text-white">Stage I: Initial Solution (V = 0 mL)</span>
            <p class="text-slate-400">
              For a strong monoprotic acid (HCl), [H+] = Ca. For a weak acid (HA), ionization equilibrium governs:
            </p>
            <p class="font-mono text-cyan-300">[H+] = sqrt(Ka * Ca)</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="font-bold text-white">Stage II: The Buffer Plateau (0 &lt; V &lt; Veq)</span>
            <p class="text-slate-400">
              The addition of strong base converts weak acid (HA) into its conjugate base (A-). This creates a buffer system modeled by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation:
            </p>
            <p class="font-mono text-cyan-300">pH = pKa + log10([A-] / [HA])</p>
            <p class="text-[11px] text-amber-300/80">At V = Veq / 2, [A-] = [HA], meaning pH = pKa.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="font-bold text-white">Stage III: The Stoichiometric Equivalence Point (V = Veq)</span>
            <p class="text-slate-400">
              All initial analyte has undergone stoichiometric conversion: Veq = (Ca * Va) / Cb. For weak acids, the remaining conjugate base hydrolyzes water:
            </p>
            <p class="font-mono text-cyan-300">A- + H2O &lt;=&gt; HA + OH-</p>
            <p class="text-[11px] text-pink-300/80">Consequently, equivalence pH for weak acids is alkaline (pH &gt; 7).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 space-y-1.5">
            <span class="font-bold text-white">Stage IV: Excess Titrant Post-Equivalence (V &gt; Veq)</span>
            <p class="text-slate-400">
              Conjugate base hydrolysis contribution becomes negligible compared to unreacted hydroxide ions added from the burette:
            </p>
            <p class="font-mono text-cyan-300">[OH-] = (Moles_titrant - Moles_analyte) / Total_Volume</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-cyan-300">2. First-Derivative Peak Analytics (dpH / dV)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm">
          In physical laboratories, experimental noise and weak buffer slopes can obscure the inflection point on a standard sigmoidal curve. Analytical chemists utilize the numerical central first derivative:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs text-center text-cyan-300">
          (dpH / dV)_i = (pH_(i+1) - pH_(i-1)) / (V_(i+1) - V_(i-1))
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
          The point of maximum rate of pH change corresponds to the mathematical inflection point. The peak of this derivative plot marks the stoichiometric equivalence volume without relying on subjective color observations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-cyan-300">3. Scientific Indicator Selection Principles</h3>
        <p class="text-sm">
          A chemical acid-base indicator is itself a weak organic dye whose protonated and deprotonated forms display distinct optical absorption spectra. To avoid systemic titration error:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-xs space-y-1.5 text-slate-400">
          <li>The indicator transition range must match the steep vertical inflection rise of the titration curve.</li>
          <li>For weak acid vs. strong base titrations (equivalence pH 8.0 to 9.5), <strong>Phenolphthalein</strong> (8.2 to 10.0) is ideal. Indicators transitioning below pH 7 change color prematurely.</li>
          <li>For weak base vs. strong acid titrations (equivalence pH 4.5 to 6.0), <strong>Methyl Orange</strong> (3.1 to 4.4) or <strong>Methyl Red</strong> (4.4 to 6.2) is appropriate.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-800">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs">
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 space-y-1">
            <span class="font-bold text-slate-200">How do you prepare an accurate primary standard for titration?</span>
            <p class="text-slate-400">
              Primary standards such as Potassium Hydrogen Phthalate (KHP, MW 204.22 g/mol) or anhydrous Sodium Carbonate (Na2CO3) must be dried in a laboratory oven at 110 deg C for 2 hours, cooled inside a desiccator, and weighed on an analytical balance to 0.1 mg precision before dissolution.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 space-y-1">
            <span class="font-bold text-slate-200">Why must dissolved CO2 be removed when titrating dilute weak acids?</span>
            <p class="text-slate-400">
              Atmospheric carbon dioxide dissolves in water to form carbonic acid (H2CO3), which consumes sodium hydroxide titrant and shifts the apparent endpoint. For analytical precision, deionized water should be boiled or purged with inert nitrogen.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
